==============================================================================
SECTION 0 -- data sanity and quantisation (ground rule 2)
==============================================================================
  20260830_094820_fan_on_cw_50k_60s_0.npz      fs= 50000   60.0 s  rms= 118.3 uV  codes=4  top code=0.862
  20260830_095020_fan_on_cw_50k_60s_1.npz      fs= 50000   60.0 s  rms= 108.1 uV  codes=4  top code=0.892
  20260829_061237_empty_baseline_60s.npz       fs= 50000   59.8 s  rms= 158.4 uV  codes=4  top code=0.681
  20260830_093545_fan_on_cw0_100k.npz          fs=100000   25.0 s  rms= 157.6 uV  codes=5  top code=0.743
  20260830_093545_fan_on_cw1_100k.npz          fs=100000   25.0 s  rms= 153.5 uV  codes=5  top code=0.773
  smoke_static.npz                             fs=100000    6.0 s  rms= 173.6 uV  codes=5  top code=0.548
  measured LSB = 336.0 uV (matches the 5 Vpp / 336 uV figure).
  The fan-on captures put 86-89% of all samples on ONE ADC code; the
  fan-off baseline straddles two (68/32). The CW IF is a ~1.2-bit signal
  and the fan-on records are the MORE severely quantised of the two.
  Mains false-tag rate at +-1.0 Hz on 60 Hz multiples: 3.3% of random frequencies (uniform in f).

==============================================================================
SECTION 1 -- POSITIVE CONTROL: the moving person (MTI finds it at ~12 sigma)
==============================================================================
  No CW capture of a person exists in this dataset, so the control runs the
  IDENTICAL CVD estimator on a range-time surface: consecutive-chirp
  cancellation (the FMCW analogue of the CW 20 Hz high-pass), range FFT,
  then FFT along slow time per range bin, summed across range bins.
  moving 240 chirps @ 50 Hz = 4.80 s | static 160 chirps = 3.20 s
  1a MTI level check (11 + 7 blocks of 20 chirps):
     0.83 m  moving -36.4 dB  static -40.5 dB  diff +4.08 dB  t=  9.3 sigma
     1.67 m  moving -30.8 dB  static -37.1 dB  diff +6.23 dB  t= 13.6 sigma
     2.50 m  moving -31.0 dB  static -37.6 dB  diff +6.55 dB  t=  9.3 sigma
     3.33 m  moving -34.5 dB  static -37.2 dB  diff +2.68 dB  t=  4.4 sigma
     4.16 m  moving -38.5 dB  static -40.3 dB  diff +1.77 dB  t=  3.2 sigma
     5.00 m  moving -40.8 dB  static -42.1 dB  diff +1.34 dB  t=  3.0 sigma
  --> reproduces FINDINGS 5.5 (+6.7 dB at 1.5 m, 12 sigma). Pipeline correct.
  1b CVD cadence-line statistic, same data:
     moving  wide search   2.09 Hz z= 3.01 (117 bins) | gait band  2.09 Hz z= 1.65 (11 bins)
     static  wide search   5.03 Hz z= 4.12 ( 78 bins) | gait band  2.52 Hz z= 1.34 (7 bins)
  1c half-record repeatability (the FINDINGS 5.5 criterion: a real target
     reappears in the SAME bin in an independent run):
     moving  half0 3.78 Hz z=2.38 | half1 2.94 Hz z=2.06 | half-half corr +0.291
     static  half0 2.53 Hz z=1.33 | half1 2.53 Hz z=1.88 | half-half corr +0.414
  --> POSITIVE CONTROL FAIL: CVD peak on the person is 3.01 sigma at 2.09 Hz,
      while the empty room over the same search gives 4.12 sigma. The person
      IS in this file (1a: 13.6 sigma by MTI) but the CADENCE statistic does
      not resolve it in 4.8 s / 10 gait cycles at a 50 Hz slow-time rate.

==============================================================================
SECTION 2 -- METHOD SENSITIVITY: synthetic rotor injected into the empty room
==============================================================================
  Because the person control failed, the CVD estimator needs its own control:
  inject a known periodic blade flash into the fan-OFF record, re-quantise to
  the 336 uV LSB, and find the level at which the CVD calls it.
  injected rotor: 87.0 Hz blade-pass, 1600 Hz (9.94 m/s) Doppler, 12% duty
  amp(uV)  amp/LSB  rotor rms   rms re record   CVD peak(Hz)      z   verdict
       0    0.000       0.0 uV        +nan dB         0.50     5.73   -
      20    0.060       4.3 uV       -31.4 dB         0.50     5.73   -
      40    0.119       8.5 uV       -25.4 dB         0.50     5.73   -
      60    0.179      12.8 uV       -21.9 dB         0.50     5.73   -
      80    0.238      17.0 uV       -19.4 dB         0.50     5.73   -
     100    0.298      21.3 uV       -17.4 dB         0.50     5.73   -
     120    0.357      25.5 uV       -15.9 dB         0.50     5.73   -
     140    0.417      29.8 uV       -14.5 dB        87.00    13.17   FOUND 87 Hz
     160    0.476      34.0 uV       -13.4 dB        87.00    34.08   FOUND 87 Hz
     200    0.595      42.5 uV       -11.4 dB        87.00    49.07   FOUND 87 Hz
     320    0.952      68.0 uV        -7.3 dB        87.00    66.21   FOUND 87 Hz
  --> CVD recovers a rotor down to 140 uV pk (0.42 LSB), 14.5 dB below the record rms.
      The estimator WORKS on this exact hardware, at this exact quantisation,
      in this exact empty-room noise. Its sensitivity floor is now measured.
  (11577 cadence bins searched, so the 5-sigma bar is the right one.)

==============================================================================
SECTION 3 -- FAN ON vs FAN OFF  (rate- and duration-MATCHED, 50 kSa/s, 59.8 s)
==============================================================================
  Using the DURATION+RATE MATCHED fan-on captures, so both conditions get
  identical averaging -- ground rule 5 is satisfied by construction, not
  by a correction.

  3a  ADC operating point -- a confound that would fake a detection:
      fan0  IF pedestal 11.7601 mV   rms  118.3 uV
      fan1  IF pedestal 11.7783 mV   rms  108.1 uV
      off   IF pedestal 13.2496 mV   rms  158.4 uV
      The pedestals differ by 1.45 mV = 4.3 LSB, so the two conditions dither
      differently and the fan-on records read 1.8-4.3 dB LOWER in EVERY band,
      including bands no fan can reach. Any absolute-level statistic here is
      an ADC-offset detector. The CVD is used precisely because per-bin
      variance normalisation makes it invariant to that.

  3b  Cadence peaks, whole 59.8 s record, mains multiples EXCLUDED:
      nps=512 hop=64 -> 781 frames/s, cadence Nyquist 391 Hz, velocity window 2.0-40 m/s
      fan0  best non-mains    0.70 Hz z= 8.60  |  incl. mains   59.99 Hz z= 29.22
      fan1  best non-mains    0.69 Hz z= 8.49  |  incl. mains   59.92 Hz z= 10.17
      off   best non-mains    0.50 Hz z= 5.73  |  incl. mains   60.01 Hz z= 18.63
      11577 non-mains cadence bins searched; 62 velocity bins summed.
      Every condition INCLUDING the empty room peaks at 60.0 Hz cadence: that
      is mains-synchronous modulation of the whole noise envelope, and it is
      NOT removed by notching the 60 Hz Doppler lines. It is also bigger in
      fan0 than in fan1, so it tracks the capture, not the fan.

  3c  Segment statistics (the actual test). 12 independent 4.98 s segments
      per capture, Welch t per cadence bin:
      comparison       nA   nB   max|t| non-mains   at Hz    verdict
      fan0 vs off      12   12        4.39         103.42   TEST
      fan1 vs off      12   12        4.08          29.37   TEST
      fan0+1 vs off    24   12        4.75         127.76   TEST
      fan0 vs fan1     12   12        4.69          22.94   CONTROL (same condition!)
      off_a vs off_b    6    6        5.61         156.73   CONTROL (same file!)
      962 non-mains cadence bins searched -> under a pure-noise null the largest
      |t| of 962 bins is expected near 3.71 sigma; 5 sigma is the bar.

  3d  Repeatability between the two independent fan-on captures:
      corr( fan0-off , fan1-off ) over 962 non-mains cadence bins = +0.342
      (a genuine, repeatable fan line would drive this toward +1)

  3e  The one candidate worth chasing: BOTH fan runs peak near 0.70 Hz in
      3b (z=8.6, 8.5) while the empty room peaks at 0.50 Hz (z=5.7). Two
      tests kill it.
      (i) is it in the same bin in both halves of each record?
          fan0   half0 0.334 Hz +16.4 dB  |  half1 0.301 Hz +12.3 dB
          fan1   half0 0.301 Hz +14.7 dB  |  half1 0.702 Hz +12.6 dB
          off    half0 0.869 Hz +13.3 dB  |  half1 0.468 Hz +7.9 dB
          -> it moves (0.33/0.30, 0.30/0.70, 0.87/0.47 Hz). Not a line.
      (ii) does it live at a blade velocity? A blade tip is confined to a
           velocity band; broadband drift is not.
          velocity window        fan0     fan1      off   (dB over median)
            0.2 -   1.0 m/s     +18.91    +19.07    +15.77
            1.0 -   3.0 m/s     +15.90    +14.70    +13.28
            3.0 -  10.0 m/s     +13.34    +14.74    +11.43
           10.0 -  25.0 m/s     +14.02    +12.59    +10.80
           25.0 -  60.0 m/s     +12.67    +12.45    +9.51
           60.0 - 124.0 m/s     +12.28    +12.66    +9.93
          -> flat across every velocity, INCLUDING 60-124 m/s, which no fan
             blade can reach. It is low-frequency envelope drift, not a target.

